You can drive the colour of a **circle** layer with an expression that looks at a numeric property (e.g. `population`) on each feature.  
The most common way is to use an **interpolate** expression on the `circle-color` paint property.  
Below is a minimal example that shows the whole flow:

```js
// 1️⃣  Add the source (GeoJSON, vector tiles, etc.)
map.addSource('places', {
  type: 'geojson',
  data: 'https://example.com/places.geojson'   // each feature has a `population` property
});

// 2️⃣  Add the circle layer
map.addLayer({
  id: 'places-circles',
  type: 'circle',
  source: 'places',
  // optional: if the source is vector, you also need `source-layer`
  // source-layer: 'places',
  paint: {
    // Size can also be data-driven – here we keep it constant
    'circle-radius': 6,

    // 3️⃣  Colour based on population (bigger → darker)
    //    Adjust the colour stops to suit your data range.
    'circle-color': [
      'interpolate',
      ['linear'],               // interpolation type
      ['get', 'population'],    // the numeric property
      // stop values (population) → colour (hex or rgba)
      0,    '#f2f0f7',   // very low → light
      1000, '#cbc9e2',
      5000, '#9e9ac8',
      20000,'#756bb1',
      50000,'#54278f'    // very high → dark
    ],

    // Optional: add a subtle outline so circles stay visible on dark backgrounds
    'circle-stroke-color': '#fff',
    'circle-stroke-width': 1
  }
});
```

### How it works

| Piece | What it does |
|-------|--------------|
| `['get', 'population']` | Pulls the `population` value from each feature’s properties. |
| `['interpolate', ['linear'], …]` | Creates a smooth gradient between the colour stops. `linear` means the colour changes proportionally to the numeric value. |
| The list of **stop values** (`0`, `1000`, `5000`, …) | Define the population thresholds at which the colour changes. Choose stops that match the distribution of your data. |
| Colours (`'#f2f0f7'`, `'#54278f'`) | Light → dark palette; you can use any colour scheme (e.g., a sequential palette from ColorBrewer, Viridis, etc.). |

### Tips for a good result

1. **Pick stops that reflect your data distribution** – if most features are between 1 k and 10 k, put more stops in that range for finer granularity.
2. **Use a perceptually uniform palette** – sequential palettes (e.g., Blues, Purples) work well for “bigger = darker”. Tools like [ColorBrewer](https://colorbrewer2.org/) or the `chroma-js` library can generate suitable hex values.
3. **Clamp extreme values** – if you have outliers, you can add a final stop at the maximum you want to display, and values above that will stay at the darkest colour.
4. **Combine with size** – you can also make the circles larger for higher populations using a similar `interpolate` on `circle-radius`:

   ```js
   'circle-radius': [
     'interpolate',
     ['linear'],
     ['get', 'population'],
     0,    4,
     50000, 12
   ]
   ```

5. **Performance** – Data-driven styling works fine for typical vector-tile or GeoJSON sources. If you have millions of points, consider simplifying the dataset or using clustering.

### Full example (HTML)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Population-based circle colour</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>#map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any base style
    center: [-122.44, 37.76],
    zoom: 10
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    // Add the source (replace with your own URL or data)
    map.addSource('places', {
      type: 'geojson',
      data: 'https://example.com/places.geojson'
    });

    // Add the circle layer with colour based on population
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'places-circles',
      type: 'circle',
      source: 'places',
      paint: {
        'circle-radius': 6,
        'circle-color': [
          'interpolate',
          ['linear'],
          ['get', 'population'],
          0,    '#f2f0f7',
          1000, '#cbc9e2',
          5000, '#9e9ac8',
          20000,'#756bb1',
          50000,'#54278f'
        ],
        'circle-stroke-color': '#fff',
        'circle-stroke-width': 1
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Replace the `data` URL with your own dataset and adjust the stop values/colours to match the population range you’re visualising. The circles will automatically become darker as the `population` property grows.
